# Asia-Pacific and other markets: the rate decisions and minutes of the central banks of Japan, China, Korea, India,
# Malaysia, Indonesia, Thailand, Singapore, the Philippines, Taiwan, Australia, New Zealand and Hong Kong; Japan's
# GDP release; China's Spring Festival holiday and its make-up working days; the Muhurat trading session of the
# National Stock Exchange of India; Yom Kippur at the Tel Aviv Stock Exchange; the quarterly MSCI index review; and
# the closing days of two payment systems: BOJ-NET (Japanese yen) and RITS (Australian dollar).
#
# Translated from holidays-events rules/catalog/apac_other.yaml.
#
# How to read a rule: "rule <key> for <event> in <scope> = <dates> with kind = ... cites ...;" says on which days the
# event <event> happens for the market, country or institution <scope>. The part after "=" is a date expression that
# is evaluated once per year: "31 DEC" is 31 December, "EASTER - 2D" two days before Easter Sunday, "2 MON JUN" the
# second Monday of June, "4 THU FEB" the fourth Thursday of February, "1 ZHENGYUE" the first day of the first month
# of the Chinese lunisolar calendar (Chinese New Year), "10 TISHRI" the tenth day of the Hebrew month Tishri,
# "[a : 1D : b]" every day from a to b, "{SAT + 2D, SUN + 1D}" moves a date that falls on a Saturday by two days and
# one on a Sunday by one day, "> PRECEDING" moves a date that is not a business day of the rule's scope to the
# business day before, "> FOLLOWING" to the business day after, "> EOM" to the last day of the month, "> NEXT TUE" to
# the next Tuesday strictly after the date (so "24 SEP - 1D > NEXT TUE" is the Tuesday on or after 24 September), and
# "@other.rule + 1D" is the day after each date of another rule. Rules only project: the dates an authority publishes
# are data that replace a rule's dates for that year, and dates that are only ever published have no rule.
# "kind = closed" marks a day on which the market or institution is shut; "working_day" a weekend day that is worked;
# "short_day" a day with a shortened or special session; "event" a dated event (a decision, a release, minutes, an
# index review) that does not change whether the day is a business day. "precision = month" says that only the month
# is reliable and the day is an estimate. "session" gives the local time (start - end, same time for a single
# announcement time). "cites" names the source the rule is taken from; "label official" means that source is the
# authority itself. The full syntax is in README.md in this directory. Weekends are declared in weekends.rules (every
# scope here has the Saturday-Sunday weekend except the Tel Aviv Stock Exchange, which traded Sunday to Thursday
# until 4 January 2026).
#
# Projected meeting dates: many central banks publish their meeting dates only a year or so ahead. For years not yet
# published, holidays-events projects a date only where the bank itself states a pattern or where an observed pattern
# hit the bank's own dates exactly at least 80% of the time over at least two years. Where only the month is stable,
# the rule gives one "representative" day in that month (usually the median of the past days), moved to the business
# day before when it falls on a weekend, and marks the rule "precision = month".
#
# Scopes used in this file (time zone in brackets):
#   central_bank:BOJ           Bank of Japan, Japan's central bank (Asia/Tokyo).
#   statistics_office:ESRI     Cabinet Office of Japan, Economic and Social Research Institute: compiles Japan's
#                              national accounts (GDP) (Asia/Tokyo).
#   country:CN                 China (mainland), country (Asia/Shanghai).
#   central_bank:PBOC          People's Bank of China, China's central bank (Asia/Shanghai).
#   exchange:XNSE              National Stock Exchange of India, Mumbai, stock exchange (market identifier code XNSE)
#                              (Asia/Kolkata).
#   exchange:XTAE              Tel Aviv Stock Exchange, stock exchange (market identifier code XTAE) (Asia/Jerusalem).
#   exchange:XNYS              New York Stock Exchange, stock exchange (market identifier code XNYS)
#                              (America/New_York); used here as the business-day calendar of the MSCI index reviews.
#   central_bank:BOK           Bank of Korea, South Korea's central bank (Asia/Seoul).
#   central_bank:RBI           Reserve Bank of India, India's central bank (Asia/Kolkata).
#   central_bank:BNM           Bank Negara Malaysia, Malaysia's central bank (Asia/Kuala_Lumpur).
#   central_bank:BI            Bank Indonesia, Indonesia's central bank (Asia/Jakarta).
#   central_bank:BOT           Bank of Thailand, Thailand's central bank (Asia/Bangkok).
#   central_bank:MAS           Monetary Authority of Singapore, Singapore's central bank (Asia/Singapore).
#   central_bank:BSP           Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas, the central bank of the Philippines (Asia/Manila).
#   central_bank:CBC           Central Bank of the Republic of China (Taiwan) (Asia/Taipei).
#   central_bank:RBA           Reserve Bank of Australia, Australia's central bank (Australia/Sydney).
#   central_bank:RBNZ          Reserve Bank of New Zealand, New Zealand's central bank (Pacific/Auckland).
#   central_bank:HKMA          Hong Kong Monetary Authority, Hong Kong's central banking institution
#                              (Asia/Hong_Kong).
#   settlement_system:BOJNET   BOJ-NET, the Bank of Japan's real-time gross settlement system for Japanese yen
#                              (Asia/Tokyo); in operation since 1 October 1988.
#   settlement_system:RITS     RITS (Reserve Bank Information and Transfer System), the Reserve Bank of Australia's
#                              real-time gross settlement system for Australian dollars (Australia/Sydney).


# ===================================================================================================================
# Bank of Japan (central_bank:BOJ)
# ===================================================================================================================

# Bank of Japan Monetary Policy Meeting: the day the Bank of Japan announces its monetary policy decision (the
# second day of a two-day meeting). The Bank publishes a meeting table for the current and the next year (source
# boj_mpm_schedule); those dates always win. The Bank states that it holds eight meetings a year; from 2022 to 2027
# the decision fell in January, March, April, June, July, September, October and December in 47 of 48 meetings. The
# day within the month varies (from the 17th to the 31st), so beyond the published table only the month is projected,
# on a representative day (the median of past decision days): 22 January, 18 March, 28 April, 16 June, 29 July,
# 21 September, 30 October and 19 December, each moved to the business day before if it falls on a weekend. A
# decision does not close any market.
rule boj.mpm_decision for boj_decision in central_bank:BOJ
  = 22 JAN > PRECEDING, 18 MAR > PRECEDING, 28 APR > PRECEDING, 16 JUN > PRECEDING,
         29 JUL > PRECEDING, 21 SEP > PRECEDING, 30 OCT > PRECEDING, 19 DEC > PRECEDING
  with kind = event, precision = month,
       note "Meeting table for the current and next year; projected months: Jan, Mar, Apr, Jun, Jul, Sep, Oct, Dec (day estimated)"
  cites "Bank of Japan Monetary Policy Meeting schedule, Bank of Japan" url "https://www.boj.or.jp/en/mopo/mpmsche_minu/index.htm" label official;


# ===================================================================================================================
# Cabinet Office of Japan, Economic and Social Research Institute (statistics_office:ESRI)
# ===================================================================================================================

# Japan GDP release: the quarterly first or second preliminary estimate of Japan's gross domestic product, published
# by the Cabinet Office's Economic and Social Research Institute at 8:50 Tokyo time. The institute publishes a release
# schedule covering the estimates of the next two quarters (source fd_esri_cao_go_jp). No pattern is projected:
# there are no dates until the schedule names them. A release does not close any market.
# No rule: the dates are only announced (fd_esri_cao_go_jp); they are data for the evaluator, under the key
# esri.gdp.


# ===================================================================================================================
# China (country:CN)
# The public holidays of mainland China are set by the State Council's regulation on national holidays. Each year
# (usually in early November) the General Office of the State Council publishes a notice for the next year that
# fixes the actual days off per holiday, including bridge days, and the weekend days that are worked instead
# ("make-up working days"); mainland exchanges stay closed on those make-up working days.
# ===================================================================================================================

# Make-up working days: weekend days (Saturdays or Sundays) that are declared normal working days to make up for
# bridge days added to a holiday. They are fixed only by the State Council General Office's yearly notice (source
# cn_state_council_holidays), so there are no dates until the notice is published. Banks and offices work on these
# days; the mainland stock exchanges nevertheless stay closed.
# No rule: the dates are only announced (cn_state_council_holidays); they are data for the evaluator, under the key
# cn.makeup_workday.

# Chinese New Year (Spring Festival): the statutory days off only. The State Council notice each November adds
# bridge days and make-up working days, which are not part of this rule.
# Until the end of 2024 the national holidays regulation gave the first three days of the first lunar month (day 1
# to day 3 of Zhengyue in the Chinese lunisolar calendar).
rule cn.chinese_new_year for chinese_new_year in country:CN valid to 2024-12-31
  = [1 ZHENGYUE : 1D : 1 ZHENGYUE + 2D]
  with kind = closed, note "National holidays regulation: first three days of the first lunar month"
  cites "State Council General Office notice on the arrangement of public holidays, General Office of the State Council of the People's Republic of China" url "https://www.gov.cn/zhengce/" label official;

# Chinese New Year (Spring Festival) from 2025: the regulation as amended in 2024 adds Spring Festival Eve (the last
# day of the old lunar year, the day before the first day of the first lunar month), so the statutory holiday is
# four days: the eve plus the first three days of the new year.
rule cn.chinese_new_year for chinese_new_year in country:CN valid from 2025-01-01
  = [1 ZHENGYUE - 1D : 1D : 1 ZHENGYUE + 2D]
  with kind = closed, note "National holidays regulation as amended in 2024: Spring Festival eve plus the first three days"
  cites "State Council General Office notice on the arrangement of public holidays, General Office of the State Council of the People's Republic of China" url "https://www.gov.cn/zhengce/" label official;


# ===================================================================================================================
# People's Bank of China (central_bank:PBOC)
# ===================================================================================================================

# Loan Prime Rate (LPR) fixing: China's benchmark lending rate, published by the National Interbank Funding Center
# (China Foreign Exchange Trade System, CFETS) under the authorisation of the People's Bank of China on the 20th of
# every month, or on the next business day if the 20th is a weekend day. Only weekends are counted here: Chinese
# public holidays are not taken into account by this rule. The fixing does not close any market. The catalog rule
# names no source; the CFETS LPR page, where the fixings are published, is cited.
rule pboc.lpr for pboc_lpr in central_bank:PBOC
  = 20 JAN > FOLLOWING, 20 FEB > FOLLOWING, 20 MAR > FOLLOWING, 20 APR > FOLLOWING, 20 MAY > FOLLOWING,
    20 JUN > FOLLOWING, 20 JUL > FOLLOWING, 20 AUG > FOLLOWING, 20 SEP > FOLLOWING, 20 OCT > FOLLOWING,
    20 NOV > FOLLOWING, 20 DEC > FOLLOWING
  with kind = event
  cites "Loan Prime Rate (LPR), National Interbank Funding Center (China Foreign Exchange Trade System)" url "https://www.chinamoney.com.cn/english/bmklpr/" label official;


# ===================================================================================================================
# National Stock Exchange of India (exchange:XNSE)
# ===================================================================================================================

# Muhurat trading session: a short symbolic trading session (about an hour, usually in the evening) on Diwali
# (Lakshmi Puja), a day on which the exchange is otherwise closed; it marks the start of the new Hindu accounting
# year. The NSE announces the date and times by circular a few weeks before Diwali (source apac_exchange_circulars,
# the exchanges' circulars), so there are no dates until then. The catalog files it as a session change; the kind is
# "special_session": a short session on a day that stays closed (no settlement takes place).
# No rule: the dates are only announced (apac_exchange_circulars); they are data for the evaluator, under the key
# xnse.muhurat_session.


# ===================================================================================================================
# Tel Aviv Stock Exchange (exchange:XTAE)
# ===================================================================================================================

# Yom Kippur (Day of Atonement): the 10th day of the Hebrew month Tishri, together with its eve (the 9th), on which
# the Tel Aviv Stock Exchange is also closed. The Hebrew day begins at sunset; the two Gregorian days given are the
# eve and the day itself. The catalog rule names no source.
rule xtae.yom_kippur for yom_kippur in exchange:XTAE = [10 TISHRI - 1D : 1D : 10 TISHRI]
  with kind = closed;


# ===================================================================================================================
# MSCI index review (exchange:XNYS)
# ===================================================================================================================

# MSCI index review effective: MSCI's quarterly index reviews (February, May, August and November) take effect as of
# the close of the last business day of the month. The rule takes the last business day of the New York Stock
# Exchange calendar: the last day of the month, moved back past weekends and NYSE holidays (the NYSE holidays are in
# us.rules). The review does not close any market. The catalog rule names no source.
rule msci.rebalance for msci_rebalance in exchange:XNYS
  = 1 FEB > EOM > PRECEDING, 1 MAY > EOM > PRECEDING, 1 AUG > EOM > PRECEDING, 1 NOV > EOM > PRECEDING
  with kind = event;


# ===================================================================================================================
# Bank of Korea (central_bank:BOK)
# ===================================================================================================================

# Bank of Korea Monetary Policy Board rate decision. The Bank publishes next year's eight meeting dates by press
# release in late October (source bok_mpb_schedule); those dates always win. Observed since 2017: eight meetings, in
# January, February, April, May, July, August, October and November. The meetings that come with the economic
# outlook (February, May, August, November) fall on the fourth Thursday of the month (21 of 24 cases from 2021 to
# 2026); they are projected on that day, or the business day before if it is not one. The January, April, July and
# October meetings vary and are projected only by month, on the 15th (moved to the business day before if it is a
# weekend day). Caveat: the language sets precision for a whole rule, not per date; the rule is left at day precision
# because half of its projected dates follow a day-exact pattern, but the January, April, July and October dates are
# month estimates only. A decision does not close any market.
rule bok.mpb_decision for bok_decision in central_bank:BOK
  = 4 THU FEB > PRECEDING, 4 THU MAY > PRECEDING, 4 THU AUG > PRECEDING, 4 THU NOV > PRECEDING,
         15 JAN > PRECEDING, 15 APR > PRECEDING, 15 JUL > PRECEDING, 15 OCT > PRECEDING
  with kind = event,
       note "Next year's dates by press release in late October; Feb/May/Aug/Nov projected on the fourth Thursday, Jan/Apr/Jul/Oct only by month (15th)"
  cites "Bank of Korea Monetary Policy Board meeting dates and minutes, Bank of Korea" url "https://www.bok.or.kr/eng/main/contents.do?menuNo=400020" label official;

# Bank of Korea Monetary Policy Board minutes. The Bank lists them once published; it states no fixed lag after the
# meeting, so nothing is projected and there are no dates until they appear.
# No rule: the dates are only announced (bok_mpb_schedule); they are data for the evaluator, under the key
# bok.mpb_minutes.


# ===================================================================================================================
# Reserve Bank of India (central_bank:RBI)
# ===================================================================================================================

# Reserve Bank of India Monetary Policy Committee (MPC) decision. Under Section 45ZI of the Reserve Bank of India
# Act the committee meets (in practice six three-day meetings per fiscal year, April to March); the Bank publishes the
# schedule for the fiscal year in a press release in late March (source rbi_mpc_schedule), and those dates always
# win. Meetings in February, April, June, August and December were held every year from fiscal 2022-23 to 2026-27;
# beyond the published schedule they are projected by month only, on the 6th (moved to the business day before if
# that is a weekend day). The meeting around the end of September / start of October straddles the month end and is
# not projected. A decision does not close any market.
rule rbi.mpc_decision for rbi_decision in central_bank:RBI
  = 6 FEB > PRECEDING, 6 APR > PRECEDING, 6 JUN > PRECEDING, 6 AUG > PRECEDING, 6 DEC > PRECEDING
  with kind = event, precision = month,
       note "Press release for the fiscal year (April-March), late March; Feb, Apr, Jun, Aug, Dec projected by month"
  cites "Reserve Bank of India Monetary Policy Committee meeting schedule, Reserve Bank of India" url "https://www.rbi.org.in/Scripts/BS_PressReleaseDisplay.aspx?prid=62422" label official;


# ===================================================================================================================
# Bank Negara Malaysia (central_bank:BNM)
# ===================================================================================================================

# Bank Negara Malaysia Monetary Policy Committee decision (the Monetary Policy Statement, released at 3:00 pm Kuala
# Lumpur time after each meeting). The Bank states six MPC meetings a year (Central Bank of Malaysia Act 2009) and
# publishes next year's schedule in the last Monetary Policy Statement of the year (source bnm_mpc_schedule); those
# dates always win. From 2023 to 2026 all 24 meetings were in January, March, May, July, September and November;
# beyond the schedule they are projected by month, on a representative day: 22 January, 6 March, 8 May, 9 July,
# 4 September and 5 November, each moved to the business day before if it falls on a weekend. A decision does not
# close any market.
rule bnm.mpc_decision for bnm_decision in central_bank:BNM
  = 22 JAN > PRECEDING, 6 MAR > PRECEDING, 8 MAY > PRECEDING, 9 JUL > PRECEDING, 4 SEP > PRECEDING,
         5 NOV > PRECEDING
  with kind = event, precision = month, session 15:00 tz "Asia/Kuala_Lumpur",
       note "Next year's schedule in the last Monetary Policy Statement of the year; statement released at 3:00 pm"
  cites "Bank Negara Malaysia MPC meeting schedule (in the Monetary Policy Statement), Bank Negara Malaysia" url "https://www.bnm.gov.my/-/monetary-policy-statement-06112025" label official;


# ===================================================================================================================
# Bank Indonesia (central_bank:BI)
# ===================================================================================================================

# Bank Indonesia Board of Governors meeting (RDG) rate decision, announced on the second day of a two-day meeting.
# By law the Board meets at least once a month ("the RDG shall be held at least once a month"); there was one
# scheduled meeting in every month of 2023, 2025 and 2026. The Bank publishes next year's schedule in a news release
# in December (source bi_bog_schedule); those dates always win. Beyond it one decision per month is projected, by
# month only, on the 19th (moved to the business day before if that is a weekend day). Off-schedule decisions are
# possible. A decision does not close any market.
rule bi.bog_decision for bi_decision in central_bank:BI
  = 19 JAN > PRECEDING, 19 FEB > PRECEDING, 19 MAR > PRECEDING, 19 APR > PRECEDING, 19 MAY > PRECEDING,
         19 JUN > PRECEDING, 19 JUL > PRECEDING, 19 AUG > PRECEDING, 19 SEP > PRECEDING, 19 OCT > PRECEDING,
         19 NOV > PRECEDING, 19 DEC > PRECEDING
  with kind = event, precision = month, note "News release in December for the next year; one meeting per month projected"
  cites "Bank Indonesia Board of Governors meeting schedule, Bank Indonesia" url "https://www.bi.go.id/en/publikasi/ruang-media/news-release/Pages/sp_2730825.aspx" label official;


# ===================================================================================================================
# Bank of Thailand (central_bank:BOT)
# ===================================================================================================================

# Bank of Thailand Monetary Policy Committee decision (six meetings a year, on Wednesdays). The Bank publishes a
# table for the current and the next year (source bot_mpc_schedule); no pattern is projected, so there are no dates
# until the table lists them. Special meetings are possible. A decision does not close any market.
# No rule: the dates are only announced (bot_mpc_schedule); they are data for the evaluator, under the key
# bot.mpc_decision.

# Bank of Thailand Monetary Policy Committee minutes, published two weeks after each meeting. Taken from the Bank's
# table only; no dates until listed.
# No rule: the dates are only announced (bot_mpc_schedule); they are data for the evaluator, under the key
# bot.mpc_minutes.


# ===================================================================================================================
# Monetary Authority of Singapore (central_bank:MAS)
# ===================================================================================================================

# MAS Monetary Policy Statement. MAS publishes the expected dates in its advance release calendar about four months
# ahead (source mas_release_calendar); those dates always win. MAS states that the April and October statements
# coincide with the Ministry of Trade and Industry's advance GDP estimates; they were on the 14th, or the business
# day before it, in 17 of 18 cases from 2018 to 2026, and are projected that way (only weekends are counted as
# non-business days here). The January and July statements vary and are not projected. A statement does not close
# any market.
rule mas.policy_statement for mas_policy_statement in central_bank:MAS
  = 14 APR > PRECEDING, 14 OCT > PRECEDING
  with kind = event, note "Advance release calendar, about four months ahead; April and October projected on the 14th"
  cites "MAS advance release calendar (Monetary Policy Statement), Monetary Authority of Singapore" url "https://www.mas.gov.sg/monetary-policy/advance-release-calendar" label official;


# ===================================================================================================================
# Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas (central_bank:BSP)
# ===================================================================================================================

# BSP Monetary Board policy decision (six meetings a year, on Thursdays). The central bank publishes a schedule table
# for the year (source bsp_mb_schedule); no pattern is projected, so there are no dates until it is published.
# Off-cycle meetings are possible and do not appear in the table. A decision does not close any market.
# No rule: the dates are only announced (bsp_mb_schedule); they are data for the evaluator, under the key
# bsp.mb_decision.


# ===================================================================================================================
# Central Bank of the Republic of China (Taiwan) (central_bank:CBC)
# ===================================================================================================================

# Central Bank of the Republic of China (Taiwan) quarterly Board meeting decision on monetary policy. The bank
# publishes a provisional schedule for the next year the day after its December meeting (source cbc_mp_schedule); no
# pattern is projected, so there are no dates until then. A decision does not close any market.
# No rule: the dates are only announced (cbc_mp_schedule); they are data for the evaluator, under the key
# cbc.board_decision.

# Minutes of the Central Bank of the Republic of China (Taiwan) Board meetings, published about six weeks after each
# meeting (the Chinese version prevails over the English translation). No dates until published.
# No rule: the dates are only announced (cbc_mp_schedule); they are data for the evaluator, under the key
# cbc.minutes.


# ===================================================================================================================
# Reserve Bank of Australia (central_bank:RBA)
# ===================================================================================================================

# Reserve Bank of Australia Monetary Policy Board decision, announced at 2:30 pm Sydney time on the second day of a
# two-day meeting. The Bank's calendar lists the current and the next year (source rba_calendar); those dates always
# win. Since the eight-meeting schedule began in 2024, three meetings followed a fixed pattern in all 12 cases from
# 2024 to 2027: the last Tuesday of September (the Tuesday on or after 24 September), the first Tuesday of November
# and the second Tuesday of December; the November and December dates move to the business day before if that
# Tuesday is not a business day. The other five meetings are not projected. A decision does not close any market.
# (The "session" here is the announcement time.)
rule rba.mpb_decision for rba_decision in central_bank:RBA
  = 24 SEP - 1D > NEXT TUE, 1 TUE NOV > PRECEDING, 2 TUE DEC > PRECEDING
  with kind = event, session 14:30 tz "Australia/Sydney",
       note "Calendar with the current and the next year; September, November and December projected; decision at 2:30 pm"
  cites "RBA calendar: Monetary Policy Board decisions and minutes, Reserve Bank of Australia" url "https://www.rba.gov.au/schedules-events/calendar/?topics=mone" label official;

# Minutes of the Reserve Bank of Australia Monetary Policy Board, released two weeks after each decision at 11:30 am
# Sydney time. Taken from the Bank's calendar only; no dates until listed.
# No rule: the dates are only announced (rba_calendar); they are data for the evaluator, under the key
# rba.mpb_minutes.


# ===================================================================================================================
# Reserve Bank of New Zealand (central_bank:RBNZ)
# ===================================================================================================================

# Reserve Bank of New Zealand Official Cash Rate (OCR) decision. The Bank publishes the dates about a year ahead
# (source rbnz_ocr_dates). Its site blocks automated access, and the project does not get around such checks, so
# the dates must come by another official route; no pattern is projected. A decision does not close any market.
# No rule: the dates are only announced (rbnz_ocr_dates); they are data for the evaluator, under the key
# rbnz.ocr_decision.


# ===================================================================================================================
# Hong Kong Monetary Authority (central_bank:HKMA)
# ===================================================================================================================

# HKMA Base Rate review. Hong Kong's currency is pegged to the US dollar, so the HKMA has no meeting calendar of its
# own: the Base Rate is set at 50 basis points above the lower end of the US federal funds target range (or the
# average of the five-day moving averages of the overnight and one-month HIBOR rates, whichever is higher), and is
# adjusted on the Hong Kong morning after each US Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC) decision. Hence the date is
# the calendar day after every FOMC decision, announced or projected (rule fed.fomc_decision in us.rules). The
# review does not close any market.
rule hkma.base_rate for hkma_base_rate in central_bank:HKMA = @fed.fomc_decision + 1D
  with kind = event,
       note "Base Rate = 50 bp above the lower end of the FOMC target range (or the HIBOR average if higher); adjusted the next Hong Kong morning after an FOMC move"
  cites "HKMA Base Rate announcements, Hong Kong Monetary Authority" url "https://www.hkma.gov.hk/eng/news-and-media/press-releases/2024/11/20241108-3" label official;


# ===================================================================================================================
# BOJ-NET, Japanese yen settlement (settlement_system:BOJNET)
# BOJ-NET settles on bank business days. Japanese bank holidays are set by the Order for Enforcement of the Banking
# Act (Cabinet Order No. 40 of 1982), article 5(1): (i) the national holidays under the Act on National Holidays,
# (ii) 31 December to 3 January, (iii) Saturdays; Sundays are holidays by the Banking Act itself. The national
# holidays (including 1 January) come from the Cabinet Office's holiday list for Japan and are not repeated here;
# the rules below add the remaining year-end days. They are single days rather than one span from 31 December to
# 3 January, so that 1 January (and a substitute holiday on 2 January) stays recorded as the national holiday it is.
# The days are fixed and not moved when they fall on a weekend (BOJ-NET is closed on weekends anyway).
# ===================================================================================================================

# Year-end bank holiday, 31 December.
rule bojnet.dec_31 for jp_bank_new_year_holidays in settlement_system:BOJNET = 31 DEC
  with kind = closed
  cites "Japan bank holidays (Order for Enforcement of the Banking Act, art. 5; e-Gov law data), Digital Agency, Government of Japan"
        art "Order for Enforcement of the Banking Act (Cabinet Order No. 40 of 1982) art. 5(1)(ii)"
        url "https://laws.e-gov.go.jp/law/357CO0000000040" label official;

# New Year bank holiday, 2 January.
rule bojnet.jan_2 for jp_bank_holiday_jan_2 in settlement_system:BOJNET = 2 JAN
  with kind = closed
  cites "Japan bank holidays (Order for Enforcement of the Banking Act, art. 5; e-Gov law data), Digital Agency, Government of Japan"
        art "Order for Enforcement of the Banking Act (Cabinet Order No. 40 of 1982) art. 5(1)(ii)"
        url "https://laws.e-gov.go.jp/law/357CO0000000040" label official;

# New Year bank holiday, 3 January.
rule bojnet.jan_3 for jp_bank_holiday_jan_3 in settlement_system:BOJNET = 3 JAN
  with kind = closed
  cites "Japan bank holidays (Order for Enforcement of the Banking Act, art. 5; e-Gov law data), Digital Agency, Government of Japan"
        art "Order for Enforcement of the Banking Act (Cabinet Order No. 40 of 1982) art. 5(1)(ii)"
        url "https://laws.e-gov.go.jp/law/357CO0000000040" label official;


# ===================================================================================================================
# RITS, Australian dollar settlement (settlement_system:RITS)
# The Reserve Bank of Australia states in its RITS business hours that RITS is closed on weekends and on the
# following public holidays "where they are observed in NSW and Victoria": New Year's Day, Australia Day, Good
# Friday, Easter Monday, Anzac Day, King's Birthday, Christmas Day and Boxing Day. The RBA publishes the list for one
# year at a time (source rba_bank_holidays); these rules give the following years. A holiday counts only where both
# New South Wales and Victoria observe it, so a weekend substitute day observed in only one of the two states does
# not close RITS.
# ===================================================================================================================

# New Year's Day, 1 January; on a Saturday the holiday is observed on Monday 3 January, on a Sunday on Monday
# 2 January.
rule rits.new_years_day for new_years_day in settlement_system:RITS = 1 JAN {SAT + 2D, SUN + 1D}
  with kind = closed
  cites "Reserve Bank of Australia bank holidays and RITS closures, Reserve Bank of Australia" url "https://www.rba.gov.au/schedules-events/bank-holidays/" label official;

# Australia Day, 26 January; on a Saturday observed on Monday 28 January, on a Sunday on Monday 27 January.
rule rits.australia_day for australia_day in settlement_system:RITS = 26 JAN {SAT + 2D, SUN + 1D}
  with kind = closed
  cites "Reserve Bank of Australia bank holidays and RITS closures, Reserve Bank of Australia" url "https://www.rba.gov.au/schedules-events/bank-holidays/" label official;

# Good Friday, two days before Easter Sunday (Western, Gregorian Easter).
rule rits.good_friday for good_friday in settlement_system:RITS = EASTER - 2D
  with kind = closed
  cites "Reserve Bank of Australia bank holidays and RITS closures, Reserve Bank of Australia" url "https://www.rba.gov.au/schedules-events/bank-holidays/" label official;

# Easter Monday, the day after Easter Sunday.
rule rits.easter_monday for easter_monday in settlement_system:RITS = EASTER + 1D
  with kind = closed
  cites "Reserve Bank of Australia bank holidays and RITS closures, Reserve Bank of Australia" url "https://www.rba.gov.au/schedules-events/bank-holidays/" label official;

# Anzac Day, 25 April. It is not moved when it falls on a weekend: Victoria has no Monday substitute for Anzac Day,
# so it is not a holiday observed in both NSW and Victoria and RITS stays open on that Monday (for example on
# Monday 27 April 2026, Anzac Day 2026 being a Saturday).
rule rits.anzac_day for anzac_day in settlement_system:RITS = 25 APR
  with kind = closed
  cites "Reserve Bank of Australia bank holidays and RITS closures, Reserve Bank of Australia" url "https://www.rba.gov.au/schedules-events/bank-holidays/" label official;

# King's Birthday (the Sovereign's Birthday holiday), the second Monday of June in NSW and Victoria.
rule rits.kings_birthday for kings_birthday in settlement_system:RITS = 2 MON JUN
  with kind = closed
  cites "Reserve Bank of Australia bank holidays and RITS closures, Reserve Bank of Australia" url "https://www.rba.gov.au/schedules-events/bank-holidays/" label official;

# Christmas Day, 25 December. If it falls on Saturday or Sunday the holiday moves to the next free weekday:
# Saturday -> Monday 27, Sunday -> Tuesday 27, because Boxing Day takes Monday 26. Both cases are two days later.
rule rits.christmas_day for christmas_day in settlement_system:RITS = 25 DEC {SAT + 2D, SUN + 2D}
  with kind = closed
  cites "Reserve Bank of Australia bank holidays and RITS closures, Reserve Bank of Australia" url "https://www.rba.gov.au/schedules-events/bank-holidays/" label official;

# Boxing Day, 26 December. If it falls on a Saturday the holiday is Monday 28 (Christmas Day takes Monday 27); if it
# falls on a Sunday, Tuesday 28 (Christmas Day, on Saturday 25, takes Monday 27). Both cases are two days later.
rule rits.boxing_day for boxing_day in settlement_system:RITS = 26 DEC {SAT + 2D, SUN + 2D}
  with kind = closed
  cites "Reserve Bank of Australia bank holidays and RITS closures, Reserve Bank of Australia" url "https://www.rba.gov.au/schedules-events/bank-holidays/" label official;
